module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.capability_arrives_outside_the_converged_path

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data capability_arrives_outside_the_converged_path: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "capability_arrives_outside_the_converged_path" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a host holds a capability that no modeled path put there, so every later green on that host is uninterpretable.** INVALID STATE: a host answers ready for some capability while the convergence that is supposed to be the only way that capability arrives has not delivered it. The harm is not the capability -- it usually works -- it is that a receipt naming the host can no longer distinguish the modeled path working from a hand-run answering, and the ambiguity is permanent and travels with every future citation of that host. ",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS, against neighbours it is easy to file as. It is not `execution_provenance_loss`: there an observation may never have run, whereas here the observation runs and reports faithfully -- the host really does hold the capability. It is not `head_landed_by_hand_before_its_own_verification_reported`: that is an artifact landing uncertified, this is DURABLE HOST STATE whose origin is unrecorded. The tell is a convergence that reports `already satisfied` for a host it never satisfied. ",

    "THE STRUCTURAL CAUSE IS A CAPABILITY AUTHORIZED BY ONE MODULE, DISCLAIMED BY ANOTHER, AND EXECUTED BY NEITHER, which is DESIGN section 6's out-of-band actuation wearing convergence vocabulary. `gunbc.runner.runner_host_grants` emits EnsureSupplementaryGroupMembership and `runner_host_sudoers_content` renders permission to run its argv; `gunbc.runner_microvm_host_ready` explicitly disclaims applying it, naming the result 'a hole an operator falls into once per host, by hand'. A modeled capability with no executor is a manual step, and the roster row that grants it makes the manual step look converged. ",

    "SPECIMEN A, srv2 KVM (2026-09-05): an operator ran `sudo usermod -aG kvm ghrunner` by hand, the argv already rendered at provisioning/srv2/gunbc-ghrunner.sudoers. srv2 has read kvm=writable ever since while every sibling host reads present-not-writable. The module's own annotation recording the earlier measurement went stale at that moment and COULD NOT GO RED, because DESIGN section 4c says no Accepted program reads an annotation; it was caught only because someone asked whether the grant was converged. ",

    "SPECIMEN B, srv1 Firecracker (2026-09-06, this row's occasion): a session ran the modeled `runner_microvm_host_converge_wet` by hand on srv1, which installed Firecracker v1.16.1-aarch64 into the executor-owned root -- 30 paths under ~/.local/share/gunbc-firecracker, the root directory itself created by the run, so nothing pre-existed. It was digest-verified, idempotent, and spent no host grant, and it was STILL wrong: srv1 is the canary of record, and its capability would not have come from the host-image path that is supposed to own it. ",

    "THE RULING, AND WHY DECLARING THE DEVIATION WAS REJECTED AS THE WEAKER ARM. The first recommendation was to keep the install and write it down. The operator overrode it: declaring makes the ambiguity legible but leaves every later srv1 receipt carrying a provenance caveat forever, whereas removing it makes the question stop existing. CONVERGENCE IS ONLY MEANINGFUL IF IT IS THE ONLY WAY STATE ARRIVES. Specimen B was reverted the same day -- the enumerated tree removed and nothing adjacent touched -- and the honest post-revert state is that srv1 cannot run a guest, which is what makes the convergence gap visible instead of papered over. ",

    "REPAIR, IN PREFERENCE ORDER: bake the capability into a host image so the question cannot be asked, or close it in fleet converge. NOT an applier bolted beside the disclaiming module, and never the sudoers argv by hand -- that converts a modeled hole into a second undeclared deviation, which is how specimen A was produced. The reviewer's question for any host-capability claim: which run put it there, and can you name it. ",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
